.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt BOOTBRIDGE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bootbridge
.Nd connect a network interface to bootd and emulators over UDP
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar config
.Fl i Ar interface
.Op Fl listen Ar addr : Ns Ar port
.Op Fl peer Ar addr : Ns Ar port ...
.Op Fl l2tp Ar addr : Ns Ar port
.Op Fl l2tp-peer Ar addr : Ns Ar port ...
.Op Fl session Ar id
.Op Fl cookie Ar hex
.Op Fl counter Ns = Ns Ar bool
.Op Fl stats Ar duration
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl print
.Op Fl X
.Op Fl print-iface Ar name
.Nm
.Fl version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
bridges an Ethernet interface to UDP, so that
.Xr bootd 8 ,
simh, QEMU and other emulators can exchange frames with the machines on
it.
It is the only part of gobootd that needs to capture: it must run as root,
with
.Dv CAP_NET_RAW
on Linux, or with access to
.Pa /dev/bpf*
on BSD and macOS.
.Pp
Frames travel over UDP in one of two formats, on separate listeners that
can run at once:
.Bl -tag -width L2TPv3
.It raw
One Ethernet frame per datagram, with no header, as the HECnet bridge,
simh
.Pq Li attach xq udp:...
and QEMU
.Pq Fl netdev Cm dgram
send them.
There is no authentication, so the raw listener should stay on a loopback
address;
.Nm
warns about any other address.
.It L2TPv3
Static L2TPv3 over UDP (RFC 3931) carrying Ethernet, without the control
connection, as Linux
.Pq Nm ip Cm l2tp
and QEMU
.Pq Fl netdev Cm l2tpv3
speak it.
Datagrams with the wrong session or cookie are dropped, so the cookie
keeps out anyone who does not know it.
It is sent in clear, which is no protection against anyone who can see
the traffic.
.El
.Pp
Every endpoint is a UDP address.
Fixed peers always receive frames.
Any other address that sends a valid frame becomes a peer for 180 seconds.
Unicast frames go to the peer that last sent from their destination
address, as in a learning switch; broadcast, multicast and frames for
unknown destinations go to every peer.
Frames also pass between peers, never back to the peer they came from.
Fixed peers are sent a keepalive every 60 seconds.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar config
Read the settings from an HCL configuration file (see
.Sx CONFIGURATION ) .
Options given on the command line override it.
.It Fl i Ar interface
The network interface to bridge.
Required, here or in the configuration file.
.It Fl listen Ar addr : Ns Ar port
The UDP address to exchange raw frames on.
The default is 127.0.0.1:4711; an empty string disables the raw listener.
.It Fl peer Ar addr : Ns Ar port
A raw peer that always receives frames.
Can be given more than once.
.It Fl l2tp Ar addr : Ns Ar port
The UDP address to exchange L2TPv3 frames on.
None by default.
.It Fl l2tp-peer Ar addr : Ns Ar port
An L2TPv3 peer that always receives frames.
Can be given more than once.
.It Fl session Ar id
The L2TPv3 session ID, the same in both directions.
The default is 1.
.It Fl cookie Ar hex
The L2TPv3 cookie, 8 or 16 hex digits, the same in both directions.
.It Fl counter Ns = Ns Ar bool
Whether L2TPv3 frames carry the L2-specific sublayer with a sequence
number, as Linux and QEMU do by default.
The default is true.
.It Fl stats Ar duration
How often to log interface statistics, such as
.Li 30s
(the default) or
.Li 5m ;
0 disables them.
.It Fl v
Log every forwarding error.
.It Fl print , Fl p
Print packets as they pass through the bridge.
.It Fl X , Fl dump
Print packets with a hex and ASCII dump, like
.Nm tcpdump Fl X .
.It Fl print-iface Ar name , Fl print-interface Ar name
Only print packets on one side:
.Cm udp ,
.Cm lan ,
.Cm all
(the default), or an interface name.
.It Fl version
Print the version.
.El
.Pp
When standard input is a terminal,
.Nm
reads single keys:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "p, Space, Enter" -offset indent -compact
.It Ic p , Space , Enter
Toggle printing packets.
.It Ic x , v , d
Toggle the hex dump.
.It Ic i
Cycle the printed side: all, udp, lan.
.It Ic u , l , a
Print udp, lan or all packets.
.It Ic s
Print the interface statistics.
.It Ic q
Quit.
.It Ic h , ?
Show the keys.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is HCL, the format
.Xr bootd.hcl 5
describes.
Only the listeners it declares are opened: without a
.Ic raw
block there is no raw listener.
Its settings are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic interface No = Ar string
The network interface, as
.Fl i .
.It Ic stats No = Ar duration
As
.Fl stats .
.It Ic verbose No = Ar bool
As
.Fl v .
.It Ic print No = Ar bool
As
.Fl print .
.It Ic dump No = Ar bool
As
.Fl X .
.It Ic print_interface No = Ar string
As
.Fl print-iface .
.It Ic raw Brq ...
The raw listener, with
.Ic listen
(default 127.0.0.1:4711) and
.Ic peers ,
a list of fixed peers.
.It Ic l2tp Brq ...
The L2TPv3 listener, with
.Ic listen
(required),
.Ic peers ,
.Ic session ,
.Ic cookie
and
.Ic counter .
.El
.Pp
.Fl peer
and
.Fl l2tp-peer
replace the file's lists of peers.
Errors are reported with file and line.
A file that holds a cookie should be readable by root only;
.Nm
warns if anyone can read it.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/bootbridge.hcl -compact
.It Pa /etc/bootbridge.hcl
The configuration file the Debian and Ubuntu service uses.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/bootbridge.hcl
The configuration file the macOS launch daemon uses.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 when it is stopped, 1 if it cannot open the interface or a
listener, and 2 for invalid usage or configuration.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Connect en0 to a
.Xr bootd 8
listening on 127.0.0.1:4712:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridge -i en0 -listen 127.0.0.1:4711 -peer 127.0.0.1:4712
.Pp
Accept L2TPv3 from
.Xr bootd 8
or QEMU on other machines, which need to know the cookie:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridge -i en0 -l2tp 0.0.0.0:1701 -cookie 0x0123456789abcdef
.Pp
The same as a configuration file, with a raw listener for local
emulators as well:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
interface = "en0"

raw {
  listen = "127.0.0.1:4711"
}

l2tp {
  listen = "0.0.0.0:1701"
  cookie = "0x0123456789abcdef"
}
.Ed
.Pp
Connect QEMU on Linux to that L2TPv3 listener on 192.168.1.10:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
-netdev l2tpv3,id=n0,udp=on,src=0.0.0.0,srcport=1701,\e
    dst=192.168.1.10,dstport=1701,txsession=1,rxsession=1,\e
    cookie64=on,txcookie=0x0123456789abcdef,\e
    rxcookie=0x0123456789abcdef,counter=on
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pcap 3PCAP ,
.Xr bootd.hcl 5 ,
.Xr bootd 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%R RFC 3931
.%T Layer Two Tunneling Protocol - Version 3 (L2TPv3)
.Re
.Rs
.%R RFC 4719
.%T Transport of Ethernet Frames over Layer 2 Tunneling Protocol Version 3 (L2TPv3)
.Re
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Rico Pajarola Aq Mt rp@servium.ch
.Sh CAVEATS
Full frames exceed a 1500-byte MTU as UDP datagrams and are fragmented
across real networks.
There is no loop protection: two bridges joined twice pass frames around
forever.
.Pp
Receive offload (GRO on Linux) merges bursts of TCP segments into packets
larger than any Ethernet frame before
.Nm
captures them.
.Nm
splits them again into segments no larger than the MSS the receiving
peer announced in its SYN, or 536 bytes if it did not see the SYN.
Other oversized frames are dropped and counted as errors.
.Dl # ethtool -K enp2s0 gro off
turns the merging off.
.Pp
Wi-Fi access points drop frames from Ethernet addresses that have not
associated, so bridging a Wi-Fi interface does not work for
.Xr bootd 8 's
own address; use a
.Ic pcap
network in
.Xr bootd.hcl 5
with
.Ic mac No = Qq interface
instead.
